{"tool_name":"artem","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"artem","home":"https://github.com/david-bogdan-r/ARTEM","license":"Apache-2.0","summary":"ARTEM is a tool for the superposition of two arbitrary RNA 3D structure fragments.\nARTEM doesn\u2019t rely on sequence similarity, annotated interactions, or backbone topology. Instead, ARTEM assumes at least one residue-residue match has a close-to-zero RMSD in the ideal superposition. Then, ARTEM tries all N*M single-residue matches and derives a subset of mutually closest residues as the matches for each of the single-residue-based superpositions. Finally, ARTEM returns the superpositions ordered by their size, letting the user choose the best one based on any specific requirements. ARTEM can read/write both PDB and mmCIF format.","addition_date":"2023-09-04T12:19:37.685404Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T13:47:52.648346Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Script"],"version":["version 2.0"],"collections":["3D-BioInfo-Nucleic-Acid"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:artem","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"bogdan.d@phystech.edu","name":"Davyd Bohdan","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6456-6658","typeRole":["Developer"]}],"documentation":[{"note":"README file","type":["User manual"],"url":"https://github.com/david-bogdan-r/ARTEM/blob/main/README.md"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"cmd":"python3 artem.py r=FILENAME q=FILENAME [OPTIONS]","input":[{"data":{"term":"RNA structure","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1465"},"format":[{"term":"PDB","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1476"},{"term":"mmCIF","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1477"}]}],"operation":[{"term":"Local structure alignment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0509"},{"term":"Pairwise structure alignment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0503"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"RNA structure alignment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1493"},"format":[{"term":"PDB","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1476"},{"term":"mmCIF","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1477"}]}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"ARTEM","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"febos","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkad605","metadata":{"abstract":"Understanding the 3D structure of RNA is key to understanding RNA function. RNA 3D structure is modular and can be seen as a composition of building blocks of various sizes called tertiary motifs. Currently, long-range motifs formed between distant loops and helical regions are largely less studied than the local motifs determined by the RNA secondary structure. We surveyed long-range tertiary interactions and motifs in a non-redundant set of non-coding RNA 3D structures. A new dataset of annotated LOng-RAnge RNA 3D modules (LORA) was built using an approach that does not rely on the automatic annotations of non-canonical interactions. An original algorithm, ARTEM, was developed for annotation-, sequence- and topology-independent superposition of two arbitrary RNA 3D modules. The proposed methods allowed us to identify and describe the most common long-range RNA tertiary motifs. Along with the prevalent canonical A-minor interactions, a large number of previously undescribed staple interactions were observed. The most frequent long-range motifs were found to belong to three main motif families: planar staples, tilted staples, and helical packing motifs.","authors":[{"name":"Baulin E.F."},{"name":"Bohdan D.R."},{"name":"Bujnicki J.M."},{"name":"Voronina V.V."}],"citationCount":2,"date":"2023-09-08T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"A comprehensive survey of long-range tertiary interactions and motifs in non-coding RNA structures"},"pmcid":"PMC10484739","pmid":"37471030","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1101/2024.05.31.596898","type":["Method"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Computational biology","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3307"},{"term":"DNA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0654"},{"term":"Functional, regulatory and non-coding RNA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0659"},{"term":"Nucleic acid sites, features and motifs","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3511"},{"term":"Nucleic acid structure analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0097"},{"term":"Nucleic acids","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0077"},{"term":"RNA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0099"}]},"bioschemas":{"name":"ARTEM","home":"https://bio.tools/artem","license":"Apache-2.0","version":"version 2.0","summary":"ARTEM is a tool for the superposition of two arbitrary RNA 3D structure fragments.\nARTEM doesn\u2019t rely on sequence similarity, annotated interactions, or backbone topology. Instead, ARTEM assumes at least one residue-residue match has a close-to-zero RMSD in the ideal superposition. Then, ARTEM tries all N*M single-residue matches and derives a subset of mutually closest residues as the matches for each of the single-residue-based superpositions. Finally, ARTEM returns the superpositions ordered by their size, letting the user choose the best one based on any specific requirements. ARTEM can read/write both PDB and mmCIF format.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:artem/cmd/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"sourcecode":{"interpreted":true}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"manual":true},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":6,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":"2024"},{"count":2,"year":"2026"},{"count":3,"year":"2025"}],"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkad605","pmcid":"PMC10484739","pmid":"37471030","ref_count":64,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":"1999"},{"count":1,"year":"2004"},{"count":1,"year":"2005"},{"count":1,"year":"2009"},{"count":1,"year":"2018"},{"count":1,"year":"2023"},